+## Key Design Decisions
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Edits
+
+The record store preserves each accepted edit as a complete visit revision. A revision names the revision from which the worker began editing. When submissions based on the same starting revision disagree, the system retains the competing revisions for coordinator resolution rather than automatically combining fields.
+
+Field-aware three-way merging remains a credible alternative because it could combine unrelated changes automatically and reduce review volume. It is not selected for the first release because the system cannot reliably determine whether field changes are scientifically independent. A merge rule that combines a species identification change with a separately edited cover estimate could create a plausible but unsupported observation. Whole-visit revisions instead apply the project's preservation and inspectability tenets at the cost of a larger review queue.
+
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID for each edit, submit complete visit revisions, and present a server outcome that distinguishes an accepted current revision from an accepted competing revision awaiting review. Review-export must compare competing revisions, record a coordinator's selected outcome, and exclude visits with unresolved competing revisions from the partner CSV. Record-store owns the detailed submission and resolution contract; these downstream nodes consume that contract rather than defining their own concurrency behavior.
